(window.LADDER_CARDS["44a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44a1.1","q":"Which seven techniques make a ring, and which two of 5d1’s techniques does a field add to a ring that turns about? What does the one-member technique say, and why must a field ask for 1 ≠ 0?","a":"Regroup for plus, plus-zero, negative, turn-about for plus, regroup for times, times-one, and spread on both sides; a ring turns about when turn-about for times holds as well, and a field adds that 1 is not 0 and that every member but 0 has a reciprocal; if 1 = 0 in a ring then every w = w · 1 = w · 0 = 0, so the ring has only one member; without 1 ≠ 0 the one-member set {0} would be a field","w":["Any seven of 5d1’s techniques; a field adds regroup and spread; the one-member technique says every ring has one member; 1 ≠ 0 is asked so that 1 has a reciprocal","Regroup, turn-about and spread only; a field adds plus-zero and negative; if 1 = 0 the ring has two members; 1 ≠ 0 keeps a field finite","The nine techniques of 9·cb8; a field adds nothing; if 1 = 0 the ring has no members; 1 ≠ 0 follows from the techniques alone"],"why":"The one place where the techniques alone cannot keep 0 and 1 apart."},
 {"u":"44a2.1","q":"What are the members of R[x], and how are two of them multiplied? What are plus and times in the ring of parts of a set, and why is X · X = X there?","a":"The lists of coefficients from R with only finitely many not 0, added place by place and multiplied by gathering: the coefficient of xᵏ in pq is the sum of pᵢqⱼ with i + j = k; in the ring of parts, plus is either-but-not-both, the members in exactly one of X and Y, and times is both, X ∩ Y, with 0 = ∅ and 1 = S; X · X = X ∩ X = X","w":["The members of R[x] are functions from R to R, multiplied value by value; plus is ∪ and times is ∩; X · X = X because X ∪ X = X","The members are the numbers of R with x put in; they multiply place by place; plus is ∩ and times is ∪; X · X = ∅","The members are endless lists, multiplied by gathering; plus is either-but-not-both and times is the complement; X · X = S"],"why":"In ℤ[x]: (1 + 2x)(3 + x) = 3 + 7x + 2x²."},
 {"u":"44a3.1","q":"What is a cancelling ring? What does the cancel-exactly-when technique say, and where does 2 · 1 = 2 · 4 on ℤ/6 break it?","a":"A ring that turns about, has 1 ≠ 0, and in which ab = 0 only when a = 0 or b = 0; in a ring that turns about, ab = ac with a ≠ 0 gives b = c for every a, b, c exactly when no two members other than 0 have product 0; on ℤ/6, 2 · 3 = 0 with neither factor 0, so 2(1 − 4) = 2 · 3 = 0 and 2 cannot be cancelled: 1 ≠ 4","w":["A ring where every member has a reciprocal; cancelling holds in every ring; 2 · 1 = 2 · 4 on ℤ/6 because 1 = 4 there","A ring with finitely many members; cancelling holds exactly when the ring is a field; ℤ/6 breaks it because 6 is even","A ring that does not turn about; cancelling holds exactly when 1 = 0; ℤ/6 breaks it because 2 is undoable there"],"why":"ℤ, every field and ℚ[x] are cancelling rings."},
 {"u":"44a4.1","q":"What is an undoable member, and which are the undoable members of ℤ/6? What does the finite-cancelling technique say, and at which line does it use that the ring is finite?","a":"A member with a reciprocal in the ring; in ℤ/6 they are 1 and 5; a cancelling ring with finitely many members is a field; for a ≠ 0, multiplying by a is one-to-one by cancelling, and the ring being finite is used where a one-to-one map of a finite set to itself is onto, so 1 is reached: ab = 1 for some b","w":["A member other than 0; in ℤ/6 they are 1 to 5; a finite ring is always a field; finiteness is used at the first line","A member whose square is 1; in ℤ/6 they are 2 and 3; a finite cancelling ring has zero products; finiteness is used to cancel a","A member with a negative; in ℤ/6 all six; a cancelling ring is a field, finite or not; finiteness is not used"],"why":"ℤ is cancelling and endless, and 2 has no reciprocal there."},
 {"u":"44a5.1","q":"Why is x² = 1 not enough for x = 1 or x = −1 on ℤ/8? Why does (w + b)² = w² + 2wb + b² fail for grids?","a":"(x − 1)(x + 1) = 0 gives a factor 0 only where there are no zero products, and on ℤ/8, 3² = 1; and (w + b)² = w² + wb + bw + b², which needs turn-about for times to make wb + bw into 2wb","w":["Because 8 is not a square; and grids have no squares","It is enough on ℤ/8; and the technique fails for grids because 2 has no reciprocal","Because x² = 1 has no answers on ℤ/8; and grids cannot be added"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44b1.1","q":"What three things does an ideal hold, and why are the multiples (g) of a member an ideal? What does the undoable-fills technique say, and why does a field have only two ideals?","a":"It holds 0, holds w + b whenever it holds w and b, and holds rw for every r in R whenever it holds w; the multiples rg hold 0 = 0 · g, sums rg + sg = (r + s)g, and multiples r(sg) = (rs)g; an ideal that holds an undoable member is the whole ring, since 1 = vu is in it and then every r = r · 1; in a field every member other than 0 is undoable, so the ideals are {0} and the field itself","w":["It holds 1, sums and squares; (g) is an ideal because g is prime; an ideal with an undoable member is {0}; a field has two ideals because it has two members","It holds 0, products and reciprocals; (g) is an ideal only in ℤ; an undoable member is in every ideal; a field has no ideals","It holds 0, sums and negatives only; (g) is an ideal because it is finite; an ideal with 1 is prime; a field has two ideals, (0) and (2)"],"why":"In ℤ, the ideal made by 12 and 18 is 6ℤ."},
 {"u":"44b2.1","q":"What are the members of R/I, and how are they multiplied? At which line does the honest-product technique need I to swallow multiplying?","a":"The classes w + I = {w + i : i ∈ I} of the sameness w ∼ b when w − b is in I, multiplied by (w + I)(b + I) = wb + I; if w ∼ w′ and b ∼ b′ then wb − w′b′ = w(b − b′) + (w − w′)b′, and the third line needs I to swallow multiplying by w and by b′, so that both terms are in I","w":["The members of I, multiplied as in R; swallowing is needed at the first line, to add and take away wb′","The members of R not in I, multiplied class by class; swallowing is never needed, since I holds sums","The classes w + I, multiplied by adding their members; swallowing is needed only at the last line, for sums"],"why":"With I = nℤ this is the clock ℤ/n."},
 {"u":"44b3.1","q":"What is a ring map, and what does it lose? What does the lost-ideal technique say about ℝ[x]/(x² + 1)?","a":"A map f : R → S that keeps sums, products and 1; what it loses is the members it sends to 0, and that is an ideal I, whose classes match what the map reaches by w + I ↦ f(w), keeping sums and products; p ↦ p(i) reaches every a + bi and loses exactly the multiples of x² + 1, so ℝ[x]/(x² + 1) is a copy of ℂ","w":["A map that keeps sums only; it loses its range; ℝ[x]/(x² + 1) is a copy of ℝ","A map that keeps products only; it loses nothing; ℝ[x]/(x² + 1) has zero products, since x² + 1 splits over ℝ","A one-to-one map; it loses the members it sends to 1; ℝ[x]/(x² + 1) is a copy of ℝ[x]"],"why":"The technique of ℂ comes out of dividing: x · x = x² ∼ −1."},
 {"u":"44b4.1","q":"What are a prime ideal and a largest ideal? What does the largest-ideal technique say, and why is pℤ largest when p is prime?","a":"An ideal P other than R is prime when wb ∈ P only if w ∈ P or b ∈ P, and M other than R is largest when no ideal lies strictly between M and R; R/P has no zero products exactly when P is prime, and R/M is a field exactly when M is largest; an ideal strictly above pℤ holds a w with p ∤ w, so gcd(w, p) = 1 and uw = vp + 1 by the plus-only combination, so 1 = uw − vp is in it and it is all of ℤ","w":["A prime ideal is the multiples of a prime; a largest ideal is R itself; R/P is a field exactly when P is prime; pℤ is largest because p is the largest factor","A prime ideal holds only primes; a largest ideal has the most members; R/M has zero products when M is largest; pℤ is largest because ℤ/p is finite","An ideal is prime when it holds wb for every w and b; largest when it holds an undoable member; R/M is always a field; pℤ is largest since p divides everything"],"why":"Every largest ideal is prime; (x) in ℤ[x] is prime and not largest."},
 {"u":"44b5.1","q":"Why are the even numbers not an ideal of ℚ? Why is 6ℤ not a prime ideal?","a":"They hold sums and not multiples: ½ · 2 = 1 is not even; and 2 · 3 = 6 is in 6ℤ, and neither 2 nor 3 is","w":["They are an ideal of ℚ; and 6ℤ is prime","Because ℚ has no even numbers; and 6ℤ holds 1","Because the even numbers do not hold 0; and 6 is larger than 5"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44c1.1","q":"What are the degree and the top coefficient of a polynomial? What does the degree-adds technique say, and where does (2x + 1)² = 1 on ℤ/4 break it?","a":"The degree of a polynomial other than 0 is the largest k with a coefficient of xᵏ other than 0, and that coefficient is its top coefficient; in R[x] with R a cancelling ring, the degree of pq is the degree of p plus the degree of q, and R[x] is a cancelling ring; on ℤ/4 the top coefficients multiply to 2 · 2 = 0, a zero product, so two polynomials of degree 1 multiply to one of degree 0","w":["The degree is the number of terms and the top coefficient the largest one; degrees multiply; (2x + 1)² = 1 on ℤ/4 because 1 = 0 there","The degree is the largest coefficient; degrees add in every ring; ℤ/4 breaks nothing, since 4x² + 4x + 1 has degree 2","The degree is the least k with a coefficient other than 0; degrees add when R is finite; ℤ/4 breaks it because 4 is even"],"why":"In a cancelling ring the undoable polynomials are the undoable constants."},
 {"u":"44c2.1","q":"Which single move of 9g’s division is more than adding and multiplying? What does the top-undoable technique say, and why does it make q and r the only ones over a field?","a":"The reciprocal of dₖ, the top coefficient of d; in R[x], if the top coefficient of d has a reciprocal in R, then every p is dq + r with r = 0 or of degree less than d’s, and over a field this holds for every d ≠ 0 with q and r the only ones: two answers would give d(q − q′) = r′ − r, of degree at least d’s unless q = q′, by degrees adding","w":["Taking away; every p is dq + r for every d in every ring; q and r are the only ones because R is finite","Multiplying by x; division works when d has degree 1; q and r are not the only ones","Comparing degrees; division needs p to be bare; q and r are the only ones because remainders are constants"],"why":"On ℤ/5: x³ + 2x + 4 = (3x + 1)(2x² + x + 2) + 2."},
 {"u":"44c3.1","q":"What does the remainder-is-value technique say? Which line of the at-most-n-zeros proof needs a cancelling ring, and how does x² − 1 on ℤ/8 show it?","a":"The remainder of p on division by x − r is the constant p(r); in the at-most-n-zeros proof a zero s of (x − r₁)q other than r₁ is a zero of q because (s − r₁)q(s) = 0 with s − r₁ ≠ 0, and that step needs no zero products; ℤ/8 has zero products, and there x² − 1 has the four zeros 1, 3, 5 and 7","w":["The remainder is always 0; every line needs a cancelling ring; x² − 1 has no zeros on ℤ/8","The remainder is p(0); the first line, the division, needs a cancelling ring; x² − 1 has two zeros on ℤ/8","The remainder is r; no line needs a cancelling ring; x² − 1 has eight zeros on ℤ/8"],"why":"So r is a zero of p exactly when x − r is a factor."},
 {"u":"44c4.1","q":"What does the one-maker technique say? Why is the ideal (2, x) of ℤ[x] not the multiples of one member?","a":"Every ideal of F[x] other than {0} is the multiples of one polynomial, any member g of least degree: dividing a member p by g leaves a remainder in the ideal of smaller degree, so 0; in ℤ[x] a single maker of (2, x) would divide 2, so be ±1 or ±2, and ±1 is not in (2, x), while x is not a multiple of 2","w":["Every ideal of every ring has one maker; (2, x) is the multiples of 2x","Every ideal of F[x] is {0} or F[x]; (2, x) has no maker because x is not a number","Every ideal of F[x] is the multiples of its member of largest degree; (2, x) fails because 2 and x share a factor"],"why":"The same lines give every ideal of ℤ as nℤ."},
 {"u":"44c7.1","q":"Why can x² not be divided by 2x in ℤ[x]? Why is x² + x not the zero polynomial on ℤ/2, though both its values are 0?","a":"The top of 2x · q is even and x² has top 1: it needs ½, which ℤ has not; and a polynomial is its list of coefficients, not its values: x² + x is 0 at 0 and at 1 and its coefficients are not all 0","w":["Because x² has a smaller degree than 2x; and x² + x is the zero polynomial on ℤ/2","Because 2x is not a polynomial; and x² + x has the value 1 at 1","Because ℤ[x] has no division at all; and ℤ/2 has no polynomials"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."},
 {"u":"44c5.1","q":"When do two polynomials over a finite field give the same function? Why can that not happen for two different polynomials over an endless field?","a":"exactly when the product of x − a over every member a divides their difference; over an endless field the difference would have more zeros than its degree","w":["exactly when they have the same degree; because endless fields are ordered","never; because a polynomial is its function","exactly when their top coefficients agree; because endless fields have no zeros"],"why":"44c5: the difference is 0 at every member, each zero strikes a factor x − a, and a polynomial other than 0 has at most as many zeros as its degree."},
 {"u":"44c6.1","q":"Which readings of F[x] keep products? Why does the total from 0 to 1 not keep them?","a":"the zero reading, and for each member t the reading that puts t in, with t = L(x); the total sends x to 1/2 and x · x to 1/3, which is not 1/4","w":["every reading; the total does keep them","only p ↦ p(0); the total is not straight","none; a reading cannot be straight and keep products"],"why":"44c6: L(1) is 0 or 1; if 1, then L(xᵏ) = L(x)ᵏ, and straightness gives L(p) = p(L(x))."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44d1.1","q":"What are a principal ring, an unsplittable member, a prime member and partners? What does the unsplittable-is-prime technique say, and which technique of 44b does its proof use?","a":"A principal ring is a cancelling ring in which every ideal is the multiples of one member; q, not 0 and not undoable, is unsplittable when q = wb only if w or b is undoable, and prime when q dividing wb means q divides w or b; partners differ by an undoable factor; in a principal ring every unsplittable member is prime: (q) is largest, so R/(q) is a field by the largest-ideal technique, and a field has no zero products","w":["A principal ring is a field; unsplittable and prime mean the same in every ring; partners are equal members; the proof uses the undoable-fills technique alone","A principal ring has one ideal; unsplittable means undoable; partners multiply to 1; every prime is unsplittable, by the honest-product technique","A principal ring is finite; a prime member divides every product; partners share a factor; unsplittables are prime by the lost-ideal technique"],"why":"In 44e, 2 is unsplittable and not prime."},
 {"u":"44d2.1","q":"What does the one-factoring technique say? Why does the chain (w₀) ⊊ (w₁) ⊊ ⋯ have to stop in a principal ring?","a":"In a principal ring every member other than 0 that is not undoable is a product of unsplittables, and any two such products have the same length and can be paired off into partners; the union of a rising chain of ideals is an ideal, so it is (g) for one maker g, and g lies in some (w_k), so (w_(k+1)) ⊆ (g) ⊆ (w_k) and the chain cannot rise past k","w":["Every member is unsplittable; the chain stops because the ring is finite","Every member has many factorings of different lengths; the chain stops because degrees fall","Every member is a product of primes in ℤ only; the chain never stops, which is the totally false wanted"],"why":"In ℚ[x]: x⁴ − 4 = (x² − 2)(x² + 2), up to partners."},
 {"u":"44d3.1","q":"What are the content of a polynomial and a bare polynomial? What does the content-multiplies technique say, and on which ring does its proof find the totally false?","a":"The content of a whole-number polynomial, not 0, is the greatest common divisor of its coefficients, and a polynomial with content 1 is bare; the content of a product is the product of the contents; if a prime ℓ divided every coefficient of a product of two bare polynomials, then on (ℤ/ℓ)[x] two members other than 0 would multiply to 0, and (ℤ/ℓ)[x] is cancelling","w":["The content is the top coefficient and bare means top 1; contents add; the totally false is found on ℚ[x]","The content is the constant term; bare means no constant; the content of a product is the larger content; found on ℤ","The content is the degree; bare means degree 1; contents multiply only for primes; found on ℤ/4"],"why":"(2x + 4)(3x + 6) = 6x² + 24x + 24, with content 2 · 3 = 6."},
 {"u":"44d4.1","q":"What does the prime-wedge technique say? Why are both constant terms g₀ and h₀ multiples of ℓ, and how does that give ℓ² ∣ a₀?","a":"If f = aₙxⁿ + ⋯ + a₀ has whole coefficients, n ≥ 1, and a prime ℓ divides a₀, …, aₙ₋₁, does not divide aₙ, and ℓ² does not divide a₀, then f cannot be split over ℚ into two factors of smaller degree; on the clock of ℓ, f is aₙxⁿ, so by one factoring in (ℤ/ℓ)[x] the factors are bxᵏ and cx^(n − k) with k > 0 and n − k > 0, whose constant terms are 0 on the clock; then ℓ² ∣ g₀h₀ = a₀, the totally false","w":["If ℓ divides every coefficient, f splits; g₀ and h₀ are multiples of ℓ because f is bare; ℓ² ∣ a₀ since a₀ = g₀ + h₀","If ℓ divides no coefficient, f cannot be split; g₀ and h₀ are multiples of ℓ because their tops are; ℓ² ∣ aₙ follows","If ℓ² divides a₀, f cannot be split; g₀ is a multiple of ℓ and h₀ is not; so ℓ ∣ a₀ only"],"why":"x³ − 2 with ℓ = 2 cannot be split over ℚ."},
 {"u":"44d5.1","q":"Why is failing the prime-wedge test not a proof that a polynomial splits? Why is 2x + 2 unsplittable in ℚ[x] and not in ℤ[x]?","a":"The test only says no: x⁴ + 1 fails it and cannot be split over ℚ; and 2x + 2 = 2(x + 1), where 2 is undoable in ℚ[x] and not in ℤ[x]","w":["Failing the test does prove a split; and 2x + 2 is unsplittable in both","Because the test needs two primes; and 2x + 2 has degree 2 in ℤ[x]","Because the test is for degree 3 only; and ℚ[x] has no member 2"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44e1.1","q":"What are the members of ℤ[ω], and what is the size N? What does the whole-size technique say, and why are 1 and −1 the only undoable members?","a":"The numbers a + bω with a, b whole and ω² = −5, a ring inside ℂ; the size is N(a + bω) = a² + 5b²; N(wz) = N(w)N(z), N(w) = 1 exactly when w is undoable, and the undoable members are 1 and −1: wz = 1 gives N(w)N(z) = 1 with whole sizes, so N(w) = 1, and a² + 5b² = 1 forces b = 0 and a = ±1, since 5b² is 0 or at least 5","w":["The fractions a + bω; N = a + 5b; sizes add; ±1 are the only undoable members because ω has no reciprocal in ℂ","The numbers a + bω with ω² = 5; N = a² − 5b²; N(w) = 0 exactly when w is undoable; ±1 and ±ω are undoable","The whole numbers and ω alone; N = |a| + |b|; sizes multiply only for primes; every member of size below 5 is undoable"],"why":"a² + 5b² takes the values 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 9, … and never 2 or 3."},
 {"u":"44e2.1","q":"What does the two-factorings technique say, and which gap in the sizes a² + 5b² does its proof use? Why is 2 unsplittable in ℤ[ω] and not prime?","a":"In ℤ[ω], 6 = 2 · 3 = (1 + ω)(1 − ω), all four factors are unsplittable, and no two of them are partners; the proof uses that no member has size 2 or 3; 2 has size 4, so a split into two members that are not undoable would need sizes 2 and 2, which do not exist; and 2 divides (1 + ω)(1 − ω) = 6 while dividing neither factor, since (1 ± ω)/2 is not in ℤ[ω]","w":["6 has one factoring in ℤ[ω]; the proof uses that no member has size 4; 2 is unsplittable because it is prime in ℤ","6 = 2 · 3 only; the proof uses that sizes are never odd; 2 is prime in ℤ[ω] because it divides 6","6 = (1 + ω)(1 − ω) and 2, 3 can be split further; the gap is at size 5; 2 is not prime because 2 = ω² + 7"],"why":"So ℤ[ω] is not a principal ring: P = (2, 1 + ω) has no one maker."},
 {"u":"44e3.1","q":"What is the product of two ideals? What does the ideal-product technique say, and how do the two factorings of 6 become one?","a":"IJ is the set of sums of products ij with i in I and j in J, an ideal, and (g)(h) = (gh); with P = (2, 1 + ω), Q = (3, 1 + ω), Q′ = (3, 1 − ω): P² = (2), QQ′ = (3), PQ = (1 + ω) and PQ′ = (1 − ω); so (6) = (2)(3) = P² · QQ′ and (6) = (1 + ω)(1 − ω) = PQ · PQ′, the same four ideals P · P · Q · Q′ grouped two ways","w":["IJ is the members in both I and J; P² = P; the two factorings of 6 stay different as ideals","IJ is the set of products ij only; P² = (4), QQ′ = (9); the factorings become one because 4 · 9 = 36","IJ is I + J; PQ = (2, 3) is the whole ring; the two factorings of 6 are both wrong in ℤ[ω]"],"why":"P² = (2)(2, 1 + ω, −2 + ω), and the second ideal holds 1."},
 {"u":"44e4.1","q":"Why is ℤ[ω]/P a copy of ℤ/2, and what does that make P? What does the ideal-factoring technique say, and what part of it is held in trust?","a":"On dividing by P, ω ∼ −1 since 1 + ω is in P, and 2 ∼ 0, so every a + bω ∼ a + b read on ℤ/2: two members, a field, so P is largest and so prime; in ℤ[ω] every ideal other than {0} and the whole ring is a product of prime ideals in exactly one way, up to order; the technique is held in trust, and the lines check only that no regrouping of P, Q, Q′ makes (6) another way","w":["Because P has two makers; that makes P the whole ring; every ideal has one maker, proved on the page","Because 2 is in P; that makes P prime and not largest; ideals factor in many ways, and nothing is held in trust","Because ω ∼ 1; that makes P = (2); every member factors in one way, and the case of 6 is held in trust"],"why":"The kinds of ideals of ℤ[ω] multiply like a group of two members."},
 {"u":"44e5.1","q":"Why does 2 dividing (1 + ω)(1 − ω) not make 2 divide a factor here? Why is P not the whole ring, though 2 and 1 + ω share no unsplittable factor?","a":"Dividing a factor of every product it divides is 2 being prime, and 2 is unsplittable and not prime here; and every member 2u + (1 + ω)v of P has a + b even, so 1 is missing","w":["It does divide 1 + ω; and P is the whole ring","Because 2 is undoable in ℤ[ω]; and P holds only 2 and 1 + ω","Because the product is not 6; and P is {0}"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44f1.1","q":"How is a field L that holds a field K a space of vectors over K? What is the degree [L : K], and why is [ℝ : ℚ] endless?","a":"Read members of L as vectors, added in L, and stretched by members of K, multiplied in L: the eight conditions hold because they are techniques of the field L; the degree [L : K] is the dimension of that space; a space of n vectors over ℚ is listed like ℚⁿ, and ℝ cannot be listed, so no finite n will do","w":["L is a space over K because L is larger; the degree is the count of members of L; [ℝ : ℚ] is endless because ℝ is endless","L is a space over K only when L = K; the degree is the largest power; [ℝ : ℚ] = 2, like [ℂ : ℝ]","Members of K are the vectors and members of L stretch them; the degree is [K : L]; [ℝ : ℚ] is endless because √2 is no fraction"],"why":"[ℂ : ℝ] = 2 and [ℚ(√2) : ℚ] = 2."},
 {"u":"44f2.1","q":"What is the least polynomial of α over K, and why can it not be split? What does the root-field technique say about [ℚ(∛2) : ℚ]?","a":"Putting α in loses an ideal of K[x] other than {0}, which is (m) for one polynomial m with top coefficient 1, the least polynomial; if m = gh then g(α)h(α) = 0, so g(α) or h(α) is 0, and that one is a multiple of m; K(α) is a copy of K[x]/(m) with basis 1, α, …, α^(d − 1), so [K(α) : K] = d; ∛2 has least polynomial x³ − 2, so [ℚ(∛2) : ℚ] = 3, with basis 1, ∛2, ∛4","w":["The least polynomial is x − α; it cannot be split because it has degree 1; [ℚ(∛2) : ℚ] = 1","The least polynomial is any polynomial with zero α; it can be split; [ℚ(∛2) : ℚ] = 2, since ∛2 is no fraction","The least polynomial is the one of least top coefficient; it cannot be split because K is a field; [ℚ(∛2) : ℚ] = 9"],"why":"ℝ[x]/(x² + 1) gave ℂ by exactly this."},
 {"u":"44f3.1","q":"What does the tower technique say? Why is [ℚ(√2, √3) : ℚ] equal to 4, and what basis does the proof give?","a":"If K ⊆ L ⊆ M are fields with [M : L] = m and [L : K] = n, then [M : K] = mn, the mn products uᵢvⱼ of the two bases being a basis; √3 is not in ℚ(√2), since (a + b√2)² = 3 forces 2ab = 0 and neither a² = 3 nor 2b² = 3 has a fraction answer, so the top step has degree 2 and the bottom 2: the tower has degree 4, with basis 1, √2, √3, √6","w":["Degrees add along a tower; [ℚ(√2, √3) : ℚ] = 2 + 2 = 4, with basis √2, √3","[M : K] is the larger of m and n; the degree is 4 because √6 has degree 4; the basis is 1, √6","Degrees multiply; [ℚ(√2, √3) : ℚ] = 4 because √2 + √3 is a zero of x⁴ − 4; the basis is 1, √2, 2, 2√2"],"why":"The proof shows the products build M over K and are free."},
 {"u":"44f4.1","q":"What does the degree-divides technique say? Why is ∛2 in no field reached from ℚ by putting in square roots one at a time?","a":"If α lies in a field L of finite degree over K, the degree of α’s least polynomial divides [L : K], by the tower K ⊆ K(α) ⊆ L; a field reached by square roots one at a time has degree 2ᵏ over ℚ, each step being of degree 1 or 2, and ∛2 has degree 3, and 3 does not divide 2ᵏ","w":["The degree of α is at most [L : K]; ∛2 is in no such field because 3 > 2","The degree of α equals [L : K]; ∛2 is reached after three square roots","The degree of α divides [L : K] only when L = K(α); ∛2 is in no such field because it is no fraction"],"why":"The heart of 46i: doubling a cube means building ∛2."},
 {"u":"44f5.1","q":"Why is [ℚ(√2, √3) : ℚ] not 2 + 2? Why does √2 being a zero of x⁴ − 4 not make [ℚ(√2) : ℚ] equal to 4?","a":"Degrees multiply along a tower: 2 · 2 = 4; and the degree is that of the least polynomial, x² − 2: x⁴ − 4 can be split","w":["It is 2 + 2, which is also 4, so degrees add; and [ℚ(√2) : ℚ] is 4","Because √2 and √3 are the same field; and x⁴ − 4 has no zeros","Because the degree is 2; and x⁴ − 4 is the least polynomial of √2"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44g1.1","q":"What is the bottom prime of a finite field, and why is it prime? What does the prime-power-size technique say, and why can no field have 6 members?","a":"The least count p with p · 1 = 0; if p = ab with a, b < p then (a · 1)(b · 1) = 0, and a field has no zero products; a finite field with bottom prime p has pⁿ members, where n = [F : ℤ/p], since each member is one mix of a basis with p choices for each of n coefficients; 6 is no prime power","w":["The count of members; it is prime because fields are finite; a finite field has p members; 6 is not prime","The least member other than 0; it is prime by choice; a finite field has n · p members; no field has 6 because 6 is even","The largest prime dividing the count of members; a finite field has p² members; ℤ/6 is the field with 6 members"],"why":"The p sums of 1 are a copy of ℤ/p inside F."},
 {"u":"44g2.1","q":"What does the unsplittable-builds technique say? Why is x² + x + 1 unsplittable on ℤ/2, and why is the field of four not the clock of four?","a":"If m of degree n cannot be split over ℤ/p, then (ℤ/p)[x]/(m) is a field with pⁿ members, the remainders of degree less than n; m(0) = 1 and m(1) = 1, so it has no zero and no straight factor, and of degree 2 it cannot be split; in the field of four 1 + 1 = 0 and x(x + 1) = 1, with no zero products, while on the clock of four 2 · 2 = 0","w":["Any m of degree n gives a field of pⁿ; x² + x + 1 is unsplittable because its coefficients are 1; the field of four is the clock of four","If m splits, the quotient is a field with n members; x² + x + 1 has the zero 1 on ℤ/2; the field of four has 1 + 1 = 2","If m cannot be split the quotient has n · p members; x² + x + 1 is unsplittable because 2 is prime; the field of four is ℤ/2 twice over, with zero products"],"why":"x³ + x + 1 gives a field of 8, and x² + 1 on ℤ/3 a field of 9."},
 {"u":"44g3.1","q":"What does the power-keeps-plus technique say, and why is C(p, k) a multiple of p for 0 < k < p? Why are the zeros of x^q − x different from each other, and why do they form a field?","a":"In a ring that turns about with p · 1 = 0 for a prime p, (w + b)ᵖ = wᵖ + bᵖ; C(p, k) = p!/(k!(p − k)!), and p divides the top and no factor of the bottom; f = x^q − x has slope f′ = qx^(q − 1) − 1 = −1, never 0, so no factor (x − r)² and q different zeros; and if r^q = r and s^q = s then (r + s)^q = r + s, (rs)^q = rs and (r⁻¹)^q = r⁻¹, so the zeros hold 0, 1, sums, products, negatives and reciprocals","w":["(w + b)ᵖ = wᵖ + bᵖ in every ring; C(p, k) is a multiple of p because k is; the zeros differ because q is prime; they form a field because there are q of them","(wb)ᵖ = wᵖbᵖ only when p · 1 = 0; C(p, k) = p always; the zeros of x^q − x repeat; they form a ring with zero products","(w + b)ᵖ = w + b; C(p, k) is odd; the zeros differ because f′ = 0; they form a field because f cannot be split"],"why":"So for every prime p and n ≥ 1 there is a field with pⁿ members."},
 {"u":"44g4.1","q":"What does the whole-hand technique say, and which fact of 44c3 does its proof spend? Why is the order of 1 + i in the field of 9 exactly 8?","a":"In every finite field, the members other than 0 are the powers of one of them; the proof spends that a polynomial of degree d has at most d zeros in every cancelling ring, so in every field; (1 + i)² = 2i, (1 + i)⁴ = 2 and (1 + i)⁸ = 1, and the order divides 8 and is not 1, 2 or 4","w":["Every member other than 0 reaches every other; it spends the remainder-is-value technique; the order of 1 + i is 8 because 9 − 1 = 8","In a finite field some member has order 2; it spends the degree-adds technique; 1 + i has order 4, like i","The members other than 0 are the powers of 2; it spends that ℤ/p is a field; the order of 1 + i is 8 because (1 + i)⁸ = 1 alone"],"why":"i has order 4 in the field of 9: not every member reaches every other."},
 {"u":"44g6.1","q":"Why is the field of four not ℤ/4? Why does x⁴ + x² + 1 having no zero on ℤ/2 not make it unsplittable there?","a":"On ℤ/4, 2 · 2 = 0; in the field of four 1 + 1 = 0 and there are no zero products; and no zero rules out straight factors only: x⁴ + x² + 1 is (x² + x + 1)² there","w":["The field of four is ℤ/4; and a polynomial with no zero cannot be split","Because 4 is not a prime power; and x⁴ + x² + 1 has the zero 1","Because ℤ/4 has five members; and x⁴ + x² + 1 is a square on every field"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."},
 {"u":"44g5.1","q":"What are the two cases for the counts of 1s in a field, and what does the field hold in each? Give an endless field with 1 + 1 = 0.","a":"either a least count p of 1s is 0, p is prime, and the field holds a copy of ℤ/p; or no count is 0 and it holds a copy of ℚ; the fractions of polynomials over ℤ/2","w":["the count is even or odd; ℚ in both; the reals","the field is finite or it is ℚ; ℤ/2 itself","the count is 0 or 1; nothing smaller is inside; ℂ"],"why":"44g5: n ↦ n · 1 loses an ideal (n) of ℤ, and ℤ/(n) inside a field has no zero products, so n is 0 or a prime."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44h1.1","q":"What are the members of C and of Z? What does the crowding-quotient technique say, and why does a crowding list that does not head to 0 have a reciprocal in C/Z?","a":"C is the lists of fractions that crowd together, added and multiplied term by term, and Z the lists that head to 0; C is a ring that turns about, Z is a largest ideal of C, and so C/Z is a field; a list a that crowds and does not head to 0 has |aₙ| ≥ ε > 0 from some N on, so cₙ = 1/aₙ for n ≥ N crowds, since |1/aₙ − 1/aₘ| ≤ |aₘ − aₙ|/ε², and ac − 1 is in Z","w":["C is the real numbers and Z the whole numbers; C/Z is a clock; a list has a reciprocal because fractions do","C is the lists of fractions and Z the bounded ones; C/Z is a ring with zero products; a list not heading to 0 has reciprocal list 1/aₙ for every n","C is the crowding lists and Z the list 0, 0, 0, … alone; C is a field; a reciprocal exists because every term is positive"],"why":"The class of 1, 3/2, 17/12, 577/408, … squares to the class of 2."},
 {"u":"44h2.1","q":"Why does every regular number system hold a copy of ℚ? Which line of the fractions-sit-inside technique spends no holes, and why does a world with endlessly small members fail it?","a":"1 is positive, so 1 + 1 + ⋯ + 1 is positive for any count of 1s and never 0: the sums of 1 are a copy of the counting numbers, their differences of ℤ, and their quotients of ℚ; no holes is spent on s = sup{n · 1}, the least upper bound of a bounded counting, which then gives the totally false; a world with endlessly small members has members above every count of 1s, and no such least upper bound","w":["Because every regular number system is ℚ; no line spends no holes; the worlds with endlessly small members obey it","Because fractions are defined in it by cuts; no holes is spent on the fraction-between line only; small members fail it because they are negative","Because it is finite; no holes is spent on the first line; the worlds with small members have no 1"],"why":"So in a line with no holes, a fraction lies between any two members."},
 {"u":"44h3.1","q":"What does the only-line technique say, and how is h defined? Why does h send each fraction to itself, and why is it onto?","a":"Any two regular number systems with no holes, L and L′, are matched by exactly one map that keeps sums, products and order; h(w) is the least upper bound in L′ of the fractions below w in L; the fractions below a fraction r have r as their least upper bound, so h(r) = r; and each u′ in L′ is h of the least upper bound in L of the fractions below u′, the same move made from L′ back","w":["Any two regular number systems are matched; h(w) is the nearest fraction to w; h fixes fractions by choice; it is onto because L′ is smaller","Two lines with no holes are matched by many maps; h(w) = w; h is onto because it is one-to-one","Lines with no holes are matched keeping sums only; h(w) is the least fraction above w; h sends r to r + 1; onto fails"],"why":"The cut α goes to the class of any list of fractions that climbs to α."},
 {"u":"44h4.1","q":"What does the rigid-line technique say? How does a map that keeps only sums and products come to keep order on ℝ, and why does the same argument fail on ℚ(√2)?","a":"The only map from ℝ to ℝ that keeps sums and products and sends 1 to 1 is doing nothing; every w ≥ 0 is a square, so f(w) = f(√w)² ≥ 0, and then w < b gives f(b) − f(w) = f(b − w) > 0: order is kept, fractions are fixed, and a fraction lies between any two different reals; in ℚ(√2) not every positive member is a square, and a + b√2 ↦ a − b√2 keeps sums and products while sending √2 > 0 to −√2","w":["Every map from ℝ to ℝ is doing nothing; order is kept because the map is told to keep it; ℚ(√2) fails because it has holes only","The line has two selves; order is kept because fractions are fixed; on ℚ(√2) the argument works too","The only map keeping sums is doing nothing; order is kept by the slope technique; ℚ(√2) fails because √2 is not in it"],"why":"ℂ has a second self too, the flip. The line has none."},
 {"u":"44h5.1","q":"Why is C itself not a field, though C/Z is? Why does a + b√2 ↦ a − b√2 not keep order?","a":"1, 0, 0, … is not 0 and has no reciprocal list: only the classes form a field; and the map sends √2 − 1 > 0 to −√2 − 1 < 0","w":["C is a field; and the map keeps order","Because C has no 1; and the map sends 1 to −1","Because C is finite; and the map is not one-to-one"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44i1.1","q":"What is a module over a ring, and why is every group that turns about one over ℤ? Why has ℤ/6 no basis over ℤ?","a":"A set M with an adding that obeys the four adding conditions and a stretching by members of a ring R that turns about, obeying the four stretching conditions; in a group whose combining turns about, n · g is the sum of n copies of g and −n · g its negative, and the stretching conditions are the powers technique written with plus; in ℤ/6, 6 · g = 0 for every g with 6 ≠ 0, so no list in it is free, and a basis would be free","w":["A module is a field with vectors; a group is a module because it is finite; ℤ/6 has the basis 1","A module is a ring with a basis; every group is a module over itself; ℤ/6 has no basis because 6 is not prime","A module is a space of vectors over a field only; a group is one over ℤ by multiplying; ℤ/6 has no basis because it has zero products as a ring"],"why":"Over a field every finitely made module is free. Over ℤ the clocks are not."},
 {"u":"44i2.1","q":"Which row and column moves can be undone over ℤ? What does the diagonal-grid technique say, and why does the falling size make it stop?","a":"Swap two rows; add a multiple of one row to another; stretch a row by an undoable member, which over ℤ is ±1; and columns likewise; by such moves every grid over ℤ, or over F[x] for a field F, becomes diagonal, with entries d₁, d₂, … each dividing the next; each division with remainder puts a smaller remainder at the corner, and a size, |n| or the degree, cannot fall for ever","w":["Stretching a row by any number; every grid becomes the unit grid; it stops because the grid is finite","Swaps only; every grid becomes diagonal with equal entries; it stops because the determinant is 1","Adding rows and stretching by 2; every grid becomes triangular; the size grows until it passes every entry"],"why":"The grid 4, 6; 6, 4 becomes the diagonal 2, 10."},
 {"u":"44i3.1","q":"What does the structure technique say? How does a grid of relations enter the proof, and what does a diagonal relation say about the makers?","a":"A finitely made module over ℤ, or over F[x], is a copy of Rʳ × R/(d₁) × ⋯ × R/(dₖ) for some r ≥ 0 and members d₁ ∣ d₂ ∣ ⋯ ∣ dₖ, none undoable; n makers give a map from Rⁿ onto M, and what it loses is made by the rows of a grid A, the relations among the makers; the diagonal-grid technique changes the makers and not the quotient, and a diagonal relation says dᵢ times the iᵗʰ maker is 0, and nothing more","w":["Every module is free; the grid lists the members of M; a diagonal relation says the makers are equal","A finitely made module is a copy of R/(d) for one d; the grid is the multiplying table; a diagonal relation says each maker is 0","A module over ℤ is a field; the grid is its basis; a diagonal relation says dᵢ is undoable"],"why":"That the list d₁ ∣ ⋯ ∣ dₖ is the only one, up to partners, is held in trust."},
 {"u":"44i4.1","q":"What does the clock-list technique say? Which three groups of size 8 turn about, and why is ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 not ℤ/8?","a":"Every finite group whose combining turns about is a copy of ℤ/d₁ × ⋯ × ℤ/dₖ for exactly one list d₁ ∣ d₂ ∣ ⋯ ∣ dₖ of whole numbers above 1, and its size is d₁ ⋯ dₖ; of size 8 they are ℤ/8, ℤ/2 × ℤ/4 and ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 × ℤ/2, the lists whose product is 8 with each dividing the next; the lists 2, 2, 2 and 8 differ, and every member of ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 doubled is 0, while 1 in ℤ/8 is not","w":["Every finite group is a clock; only ℤ/8 has size 8; the product of three clocks of 2 is ℤ/6","Every group that turns about is ℤ/n for its size n; the three are ℤ/8, ℤ/4 and ℤ/2; ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 is ℤ/8 by the remainder technique","Every finite group that turns about is a product of prime clocks; of size 8 there are eight; they differ because 2 is even"],"why":"Of size 72 = 2³ · 3² there are 3 · 2 = 6."},
 {"u":"44i5.1","q":"Why is ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 not a copy of ℤ/4? Why can a row not be stretched by 2 in the diagonal-grid technique over ℤ?","a":"Every member of ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 doubled is 0, and 1 in ℤ/4 is not; and 2 is not undoable in ℤ: the move cannot be undone and changes the quotient","w":["It is a copy, by the remainder technique; and rows may be stretched by any number","Because ℤ/4 has more members; and 2 is larger than 1","Because ℤ/2 × ℤ/2 has no 0; and stretching by 2 makes the grid odd"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44j1.1","q":"What does the remainder-chain technique say, and which technique of 4j does it carry to polynomials? Why is the greatest common factor of x⁴ − 1 and x³ − 2x² + 2x − 1 equal to x − 1?","a":"To find the greatest common factor of f and g over a field, divide f by g, then g by the remainder, and so on until a remainder is 0: the last remainder other than 0 is the greatest common factor, up to a constant; it carries the split-keeps-divisors technique, f = gq + r and g sharing exactly the common factors of g and r; the chain leaves 2x² − 3x + 1, then ¾(x − 1), then 0, so the factor is x − 1 with its top made 1","w":["Multiply f and g and take the least factor; it carries the prime-divides-a-factor technique; x − 1 is the factor because both have the zero 0","Divide f by g once and take the quotient; it carries the endless-primes technique; the factor is x + 2","Take the remainders until one is 1; it carries division with remainder; the factor is 2x² − 3x + 1, the first remainder"],"why":"Run backwards, the chain writes x − 1 as uf + vg."},
 {"u":"44j2.1","q":"How is the resultant’s grid built from the coefficients of f and g? What does the shared-factor-test technique say, and which technique of rung 27 finishes its proof?","a":"For f of degree m and g of degree n it has m + n rows: n rows of f’s coefficients, each shifted one place right of the last, then m rows of g’s, and Res(f, g) is its determinant; Res(f, g) = 0 exactly when f and g share a factor of degree at least 1; sharing a factor is uf = vg with deg u < n and deg v < m, a straight equation in the m + n coefficients, and a straight map loses more than 0 exactly when its determinant is 0","w":["The grid has the values of f and g; Res = 0 exactly when f = g; finished by the same-size technique","The grid has m rows of f and n rows of g; Res ≠ 0 exactly when they share a factor; finished by the spare-vector technique","The grid is f’s coefficients on the diagonal; Res is its trace; finished by the least-obeyed technique"],"why":"Res(x² + 1, x − 2) = 5 = 2² + 1."},
 {"u":"44j3.1","q":"What does the eliminate-a-letter technique say? Where do x² + y² = 5 and xy = 2 meet, and how does the resultant find the x of each meeting?","a":"If (x₀, y₀) is on both curves, then Res_y(f, g) is 0 at x₀; and if the top coefficients in y do not both vanish at x₀, every zero x₀ of Res_y comes from a shared point, perhaps with a complex y₀; here Res_y = x⁴ − 5x² + 4 = (x − 1)(x + 1)(x − 2)(x + 2), and y = 2/x at each zero gives (1, 2), (2, 1), (−1, −2), (−2, −1)","w":["Res_y is 0 at every x; the curves meet at (1, 1) and (2, 2); the resultant gives y, not x","Res_y is a number, never a polynomial; the curves meet twice; x is found by trying values","A zero of Res_y never comes from a shared point; the curves do not meet; Res_y = x² − 5"],"why":"Elimination works when nothing can be solved for a letter."},
 {"u":"44j4.1","q":"What does the meeting-count technique say, and what is a shared piece? Why can two conics meet at most 4 times, and why do x² + y² = 1 and x² + y² = 4 meet none?","a":"Two curves of degrees m and n with no shared piece meet in at most mn points, a piece being a factor of degree at least 1 common to f and g; conics have degree 2, so at most 2 · 2 = 4; for the two circles Res_y is the constant 9, which has no zero","w":["Two curves meet exactly mn times; a shared piece is a shared point; conics meet 4 times always, and the two circles meet 4 times at complex points only","Two curves meet at most m + n times; a shared piece is a tangent; conics meet at most 4 because 2 + 2 = 4; the circles do not meet because Res_y = 0","Two curves meet in at most mn points; a shared piece is a common zero; conics meet at most 2 times; the circles do not meet because they share a piece"],"why":"The exact count mn is held in trust, much higher up the ladder."},
 {"u":"44j7.1","q":"Why does the meeting-count technique not say that two conics always meet 4 times? Why do (x − y)(x + y) = 0 and x − y = 0 meet endlessly often?","a":"The technique says at most 4: x² + y² = 1 and x² + y² = 4 meet 0 times; and the two curves share the piece x − y and meet along a whole line","w":["It does say exactly 4; and the two curves meet twice","Because conics have degree 4; and the curves are the same point","Because conics never meet; and the curves meet endlessly because both have degree 2"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."},
 {"u":"44j5.1","q":"How do you test whether a is a repeated root of f? Why has an unsplittable polynomial over ℚ no repeated root in ℂ?","a":"a is repeated exactly when f(a) = 0 and f′(a) = 0; f and f′ share no factor, so 1 = uf + vf′, and a shared root would make the right side 0","w":["when f″(a) = 0; because ℂ is endless","when f(a) = 0 twice in the chain; because unsplittables have no roots in ℂ","when f′(a) ≠ 0; because slopes over ℚ are never 0"],"why":"44j5: f = (x − a)g gives f′(a) = g(a), and (x − a)² divides f exactly when g(a) = 0."},
 {"u":"44j6.1","q":"Why is the greatest common factor of f and g the same over K and over a larger field L? What follows when two polynomials over ℚ share a root in ℂ?","a":"the remainder chain over K is also the chain over L, since quotient and remainder are the only ones at each link; they share a factor of degree at least 1 with fraction coefficients","w":["because L has more roots; they are equal","because every polynomial splits over L; nothing follows","because K and L have the same members; one divides the other"],"why":"44j6: dividing over K gives a quotient and remainder that are also the only ones over L, so the chain and its last remainder do not change."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44k1.1","q":"What does the flip-undo technique say? Why is a² − 2b² never 0 for a + b√2 other than 0, and what is 1/(3 + 2√2)?","a":"In ℚ(√2), the reciprocal of a + b√2, not 0, is (a − b√2)/(a² − 2b²); if a² − 2b² = 0 with b ≠ 0 then (a/b)² = 2, and no fraction squares to 2, while b = 0 gives a = 0; 1/(3 + 2√2) = (3 − 2√2)/(9 − 8) = 3 − 2√2","w":["The reciprocal is (a + b√2)/(a² + 2b²); a² − 2b² is never 0 because squares are positive; 1/(3 + 2√2) = 3 + 2√2","The reciprocal is 1/a + 1/(b√2); a² − 2b² can be 0; 1/(3 + 2√2) = ⅓ + 1/(2√2)","The reciprocal is (a − b√2)/(a² − 2b²); a² − 2b² is never 0 because a and b are whole; 1/(3 + 2√2) = (3 − 2√2)/17"],"why":"1/(1 + √2) = −1 + √2."},
 {"u":"44k2.1","q":"What does the times-grid technique say, and where do the 2s in the grid come from? How is 1/(1 + ∛2) found by solving the grid?","a":"The grid of multiplying by β = a + bα + cα² on the basis 1, α, α² has determinant a³ + 2b³ + 4c³ − 6abc, which is 0 only for β = 0, and solving the grid for 1 gives β’s reciprocal; the 2s come from α³ = 2 and α⁴ = 2α; (1 + α)(x + yα + zα²) = (x + 2z) + (x + y)α + (y + z)α², and asking for 1 gives x + 2z = 1, x + y = 0, y + z = 0, so z = ⅓, y = −⅓, x = ⅓: (1 − ∛2 + ∛4)/3","w":["The grid has determinant a + b + c; the 2s come from the degree 2; 1/(1 + ∛2) = (1 − ∛2)/(1 − ∛4) by a flip","The grid has determinant 0 for every β; the 2s come from √2; 1/(1 + ∛2) = 1 − ∛2","The grid has determinant a³ + b³ + c³; the 2s come from doubling rows; solving gives x = 1, y = 1, z = 0"],"why":"Check: (1 + α)(1 − α + α²) = 1 + α³ = 3."},
 {"u":"44k3.1","q":"What does the first-mix technique say? Why are 1, β, β², β³ free for β = √2 + √3, and what does that make [ℚ(β) : ℚ]?","a":"To find the least polynomial of β over K, write 1, β, β², … in a basis of the field until one is a mix of those before it; that first mix, moved to one side, is the least polynomial; in the basis 1, √2, √3, √6: β² = 5 + 2√6 and β³ = 11√2 + 9√3, which needs √2 and √3 in the ratio 11 : 9 while β has them 1 : 1, so the four are free; β⁴ = 10β² − 1 is the first mix, so [ℚ(β) : ℚ] = 4","w":["Take the product of (x − β) over the basis; 1, β, β², β³ are free because β is no fraction; the degree is 2 + 2","Write powers of β until one is 0; they are free because β² is whole; the degree is 2","The first mix is β² = 5 + 2√6; the powers are free because √6 is new; the degree is 3"],"why":"The least polynomial is x⁴ − 10x² + 1, and ℚ(β) is all of ℚ(√2, √3)."},
 {"u":"44k4.1","q":"What does the coprime-degrees technique say? Why is [ℚ(√2, ∛2) : ℚ] equal to 6, and why is [ℚ(√2, √8) : ℚ] not 4?","a":"If α has degree m and β degree n over K, and m and n share no factor, then [K(α, β) : K] = mn: both divide it, so mn does, and the tower gives at most mn; √2 has degree 2 and ∛2 degree 3, sharing nothing, so 6; √8 = 2√2 is already in ℚ(√2), and the degrees 2 and 2 share a factor, so the count falls short: 2","w":["[K(α, β) : K] = m + n always; ℚ(√2, ∛2) has degree 5; ℚ(√2, √8) has degree 4","[K(α, β) : K] = mn for every α and β; 6 = 2 · 3 and ℚ(√2, √8) has degree 4 too","[K(α, β) : K] is the larger of m and n; ℚ(√2, ∛2) has degree 3; ℚ(√2, √8) has degree 2 because 8 is even"],"why":"When the degrees share a factor, the count can fall short."},
 {"u":"44k5.1","q":"Why is there no single flip that clears ∛2 from a bottom? Why can the coprime-degrees technique not give [ℚ(√2, √3) : ℚ]?","a":"Multiplying 1 + ∛2 by 1 − ∛2 only moves the root to the bottom as ∛4: the grid must be solved; and 2 and 2 share a factor, so the check that √3 is not in ℚ(√2) is needed","w":["There is one: multiply by 1 − ∛2; and the technique gives 2 + 2","Because ∛2 has no reciprocal; and √2 and √3 have different degrees","Because ∛2 is whole; and the technique works for square roots only"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["44l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44l1.1","q":"Which ten lines make a Boolean algebra? What does the short-list technique say, and which two lines does the proof of x ∨ x = x lean on most?","a":"Turn-about for ∧ and for ∨; regroup for each; spread ∧ over ∨ and ∨ over ∧; units, x ∨ 0 = x and x ∧ 1 = x; and the two clash techniques x ∨ ¬x = 1, x ∧ ¬x = 0; say-it-again, absorb and both not-through techniques follow from the ten lines alone; the proof of x ∨ x = x leans on units and clash, each twice, with one spread between","w":["The ten rows of a table; every technique needs a table drawn; the proof leans on turn-about and regroup","Turn-about, regroup and say-it-again for each, and four clash techniques; absorb is an eleventh line; the proof leans on absorb","The nine field techniques and 1 + 1 = 0; not-through cannot be proved; the proof leans on spread alone"],"why":"No table was drawn."},
 {"u":"44l2.1","q":"What is the mirror of a statement? What does the mirror technique say, and why does it need the mirror of each of the ten lines to be one of them?","a":"The mirror swaps ∨ with ∧ and 0 with 1, and leaves ¬ alone; if a statement follows from the ten lines, so does its mirror; mirroring every line of a proof turns each axiom used into an axiom only because the mirror of each of the ten is one of the ten, so the mirrored lines are a proof of the mirrored statement","w":["The mirror swaps x with ¬x; a statement and its mirror say the same thing; the ten lines are needed to draw tables","The mirror reads a statement backwards; the mirror of a true statement is false; the mirror of a line need not be a line","The mirror swaps ∨ with ∧ only; the technique holds for the parts of a set alone; it needs ten lines because ten is even"],"why":"So x ∧ x = x comes from x ∨ x = x with no more work."},
 {"u":"44l3.1","q":"How is a Boolean algebra read as a ring? What does the own-square technique say, and why does (x + y)² = x + y give xy + yx = 0?","a":"Put x + y = (x ∧ ¬y) ∨ (¬x ∧ y), either-but-not-both, and xy = x ∧ y, with 0 and 1 as they are and the negative of x being x itself; in any ring in which every member is its own square, 1 + 1 = 0 and the ring turns about; (x + y)² = x² + xy + yx + y² = x + xy + yx + y, and taking away x + y leaves xy + yx = 0","w":["Put x + y = x ∨ y and xy = x ∧ y; in such a ring 1 + 1 = 1; (x + y)² = x² + y² gives xy = 0","Put x + y = x ∧ y and xy = x ∨ y; own squares make the ring a field; xy + yx = 0 because the ring turns about","Put x + y = ¬(x ∨ y); own squares give 1 + 1 = 2; xy + yx = 0 comes from x = y"],"why":"The way back: x ∨ y = x + y + xy, x ∧ y = xy, ¬x = 1 + x."},
 {"u":"44l4.1","q":"What does the one-polynomial technique say, and why do the counts 2^(2ⁿ) settle that different sums give different tables? How does (x ∧ y) ∨ (x ∧ ¬y) ∨ (¬x ∧ y) become x + y + xy?","a":"Every table of n inputs is exactly one sum of products of distinct inputs over ℤ/2; each table is some such sum, and there are 2^(2ⁿ) sums and 2^(2ⁿ) tables, so a map of the sums onto the tables with matching counts is one-to-one; the three rows never hold together, so each ∨ is a +: xy + x(1 + y) + (1 + x)y = xy + x + xy + y + xy = x + y + xy, since xy + xy = 0","w":["Every table is many sums; the counts settle nothing; the form becomes x + y + xy by absorb","Every table of n inputs is a product of sums; there are 2ⁿ of each; the form becomes x + y because xy = 0","Some tables have no sum; the counts differ by one; the form becomes xy + x + y + 1"],"why":"At least two of three is AB + BC + CA."},
 {"u":"44l5.1","q":"What are ≤ and an atom in a Boolean algebra? What does the atoms technique say, and why does it rule out a Boolean algebra with 6 members?","a":"x ≤ y when x ∧ y = x, and an atom is a member a ≠ 0 with nothing strictly between 0 and a; a Boolean algebra with finitely many members is matched with the parts of its set of atoms, x going to the atoms below it, keeping ∨, ∧ and ¬, so it has 2ᵏ members, k the number of atoms; 6 is no power of 2","w":["x ≤ y when x ∨ y = x; an atom is the member 1; a finite Boolean algebra has k² members; 6 is not a square","x ≤ y when x = ¬y; an atom is a member with no flip; every Boolean algebra has 2ᵏ members, endless ones too; 6 is odd","x ≤ y when x ∧ y = 0; an atom is any member but 0; a finite one has 2k members; 6 = 2 · 3 is allowed"],"why":"The divisors of 30 are the parts of {2, 3, 5}."},
 {"u":"44l6.1","q":"Why is x ∨ y not x + y in the ring? Why is the mirror of a statement a new statement, not the same one?","a":"At x = y = 1, x + y = 0 and x ∨ y = 1: x ∨ y = x + y + xy; and x ∧ 0 = 0 and x ∨ 1 = 1 are twins and different","w":["It is x + y; and a statement is its own mirror","Because x + y is not in the ring; and the mirror is the statement’s flip, so false","Because ∨ is the product; and the mirror swaps nothing"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
